From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:09:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm: accept "xenpv" and its libxl-published PCI layout

Two halves of one break: the interface between Xen's libxl and the ARM Xen
machine changed across the QEMU 7.0 -> PVH consolidation, and this restores
both sides of it. Measured first on a Raspberry Pi 5 (Xen 4.21.1-pre, DomD
toolstack, DomU whose virtio devices have backend_type=qemu); qemu run
directly with -M virt and no libxl never sees either half.

1) The machine name. libxl spawns the backend with `-machine xenpv`, the name
   hw/arm/xen_arm.c carried; hw/arm/xen-pvh.c renamed it "xenpvh", and the only
   other provider of "xenpv" (hw/xenpv/) is wired into i386 targets only. Every
   domain creation that needs a qemu backend then dies with

       xl: domcreate_launch_dm: Domain N: Qdisk backend didn't respond in time
       qdisk-N.log: unsupported machine type: "xenpv"

   Register "xenpv" as a MachineClass alias (the same mechanism hw/arm/virt.c
   uses to resolve "virt" to the newest versioned machine).

2) The PCI host bridge layout. With the alias in place qemu starts and then
   fails one layer deeper:

       -device virtio-net-pci,...,bus=pcie.0,...: Bus 'pcie.0' not found

   This machine creates its GPEX only when -machine properties describe the
   windows (pci-ecam-base/-size, pci-mmio-*, pci-intx-irq-base), which this
   libxl does not pass. What it does instead is publish the exact same values
   in xenstore -- the keys the xen-troops fork's v7.0.0-xt xen_arm.c read. (To be
   precise about the provenance: upstream's arm xenpv machine arrived in
   f457df2c45, after the 7.0 release, and was renamed "xenpvh" in 733766cd37
   (8.1); the virtio_pci_host xenstore keys appear in NO upstream tree at all,
   only in the fork.) The keys:

       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/ecam_base         = "0x33000000"
       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/ecam_size         = "0x200000"
       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/mem_base          = "0x34000000"
       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/mem_size          = "0x800000"
       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/prefetch_mem_base = "0x3a000000"
       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/prefetch_mem_size = "0x800000"
       device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/irq_first         = "44"

   (values from the live board; irq_first is GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_SPI_FIRST from
   Xen's arch-arm.h). Read them as a fallback in a machine-init wrapper, only
   while pci_ecam.size is still zero, so explicit -machine properties keep
   priority and a system that publishes no layout keeps PCI off exactly as
   before.

   THOSE TWO PREFETCH VALUES ARE PRE-4.22-0008/0009 and are kept because they
   are what the board actually published at the time. With this series'
   4.22-0008 and -0009 in place libxl computes 0xc0000000 / 0x10000000 (256 MiB)
   for virtio-pci host #0 instead; ecam_*, mem_* and irq_first are unchanged,
   since -0009 leaves VIRTIO_PCI_HOST_MEM_SIZE at 0x800000. This code reads
   whichever values it finds, so it needs no change either way. The 0xc0000000
   layout has since been observed on hardware: Raspberry Pi 4, 2026-09-12, Xen
   4.22 with this series and the 128 MiB slices of the time, prefetch_mem_base =
   0xc0000000 / prefetch_mem_size = 0x8000000 in
   device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host and the guest assigning BAR 4 there.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [restores an interface for a Xen toolstack that
 predates the property-based configuration; upstream expects libxl to pass the
 machine name and PCI properties itself]
---
diff --git a/hw/arm/xen-pvh.c b/hw/arm/xen-pvh.c
index 54fd1625376..a40a3cf0976 100644
--- a/hw/arm/xen-pvh.c
+++ b/hw/arm/xen-pvh.c
@@ -6,10 +6,13 @@
 
 #include "qemu/osdep.h"
 #include "qemu/error-report.h"
+#include "qemu/cutils.h"
 #include "qapi/qapi-commands-migration.h"
 #include "hw/boards.h"
 #include "system/system.h"
 #include "hw/xen/xen-pvh-common.h"
+#include "hw/xen/xen_backend_ops.h"
+#include "hw/xen/xen_native.h"
 #include "hw/xen/arch_hvm.h"
 
 #define TYPE_XEN_ARM  MACHINE_TYPE_NAME("xenpvh")
@@ -49,6 +52,93 @@ static void xen_pvh_set_pci_intx_irq(void *opaque, int intx_irq, int level)
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * Xen's libxl publishes the guest's virtio-pci host bridge layout in xenstore
+ * under device-model/<domid>/virtio_pci_host/ -- ecam, the two MMIO windows and
+ * the INTX SPI base -- and the xen-troops fork's v7.0.0-xt hw/arm/xen_arm.c
+ * read exactly those keys (they are not in any upstream tree). This machine
+ * instead expects the same values as -machine properties, which that libxl does
+ * not pass. Read the keys as a fallback when the properties were not given, so
+ * the toolstack that spawned us can drive us unmodified. The properties, when
+ * present, win: this runs only while pci_ecam.size == 0.
+ */
+static uint64_t xen_arm_xs_read_u64(struct qemu_xs_handle *h, const char *key,
+                                    bool *ok)
+{
+    char path[128];
+    unsigned int len;
+    uint64_t val = 0;
+    void *data;
+
+    /* Relative paths resolve under this backend domain's own xenstore home. */
+    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "device-model/%u/virtio_pci_host/%s",
+             xen_domid, key);
+    data = qemu_xen_xs_read(h, XBT_NULL, path, &len);
+    if (!data) {
+        *ok = false;
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if (qemu_strtou64(data, NULL, 0, &val)) {
+        *ok = false;
+    }
+    free(data);
+    return val;
+}
+
+static void xen_arm_pci_layout_from_xenstore(XenPVHMachineState *s)
+{
+    struct qemu_xs_handle *h;
+    bool ok = true;
+    uint64_t ecam_base, ecam_size, mem_base, mem_size;
+    uint64_t pref_base, pref_size, irq_first;
+
+    if (s->cfg.pci_ecam.size) {
+        return;         /* explicitly configured; nothing to do */
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * The xenstore ops are normally registered inside xen_register_ioreq(),
+     * which the common machine init calls after this point -- so at this
+     * moment qemu_xen_xs_open() would return NULL and the fallback would
+     * silently do nothing (measured on the board without this call).
+     * Registering the ops is idempotent, and the x86 xenpv
+     * machine (hw/xenpv/xen_machine_pv.c) makes this same call first thing
+     * in its machine init for the same reason.
+     */
+    setup_xen_backend_ops();
+
+    h = qemu_xen_xs_open();
+    if (!h) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    ecam_base = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "ecam_base", &ok);
+    ecam_size = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "ecam_size", &ok);
+    mem_base  = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "mem_base", &ok);
+    mem_size  = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "mem_size", &ok);
+    pref_base = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "prefetch_mem_base", &ok);
+    pref_size = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "prefetch_mem_size", &ok);
+    irq_first = xen_arm_xs_read_u64(h, "irq_first", &ok);
+    qemu_xen_xs_close(h);
+
+    if (!ok || !ecam_size) {
+        return;         /* no layout published; PCI simply stays off */
+    }
+
+    s->cfg.pci_ecam      = (MemMapEntry) { ecam_base, ecam_size };
+    s->cfg.pci_mmio      = (MemMapEntry) { mem_base, mem_size };
+    s->cfg.pci_mmio_high = (MemMapEntry) { pref_base, pref_size };
+    s->cfg.pci_intx_irq_base = irq_first;
+}
+
+static void (*xen_pvh_common_machine_init)(MachineState *ms);
+
+static void xen_arm_machine_init(MachineState *ms)
+{
+    xen_arm_pci_layout_from_xenstore(XEN_PVH_MACHINE(ms));
+    xen_pvh_common_machine_init(ms);
+}
+
 static void xen_arm_machine_class_init(ObjectClass *oc, void *data)
 {
     XenPVHMachineClass *xpc = XEN_PVH_MACHINE_CLASS(oc);
@@ -89,6 +179,19 @@ static void xen_arm_machine_class_init(ObjectClass *oc, void *data)
     xpc->has_virtio_mmio = true;
 
     xen_pvh_class_setup_common_props(xpc);
+
+    /*
+     * libxl still spawns the qemu backend with `-machine xenpv`, the name this
+     * machine carried in hw/arm/xen_arm.c (upstream f457df2c45, renamed in
+     * 733766cd37) before the PVH consolidation. Accept
+     * it as an alias so a Xen toolstack built against the old name can drive
+     * this QEMU; the machine's own name stays "xenpvh".
+     */
+    mc->alias = "xenpv";
+
+    /* Fill the PCI layout from xenstore before the common init consumes it. */
+    xen_pvh_common_machine_init = mc->init;
+    mc->init = xen_arm_machine_init;
 }
 
 static const TypeInfo xen_arm_machine_type = {
